<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ruhr</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ruhr area, Ruhrgebiet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ruhr is an urban and industrial region in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, named after the river Ruhr, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> that forms its southern edge. From the 19th century its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mines and steelworks, in cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a>, Dortmund, Duisburg and Bochum, made it the industrial heartland of Germany. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> occupied it in 1923 to enforce the payment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ruhr.jpg" alt="Ruhr" width="320" height="379" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_map_RVR_in_Germany.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Today the republic covers 357,022 square kilometres and has 84.7 million people by the 2023 census, the most populous member state of the European Union; Berlin is the largest city and the <b>Ruhr</b> the largest urban area.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Railways spread rapidly after the line from Nuremberg to Fürth opened in 1835 and the Leipzig–Dresden line followed in 1839, and the Rhineland, the <b>Ruhr</b>, Saxony and Silesia began to industrialise.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Rhineland and Westphalia, gained in 1815, made Prussia an industrial power, with coal and iron in the <b>Ruhr</b> and Upper Silesia, firms such as Krupp, a railway network from the 1840s and the Zollverein customs union, launched under Prussian leadership in 1834, which bound much of the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Their county lay between the <b>Ruhr</b> and the Lippe, and they long fought the archbishops of Cologne for dominance in Westphalia.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-mark">County of Mark</a></li><li><blockquote>It belonged to the Grand Duchy of Berg from 1808 and to Prussia from 1815, growing into a great coal and steel city of the <b>Ruhr</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-dortmund">Free Imperial City of Dortmund</a></li><li><blockquote>During the occupation of the <b>Ruhr</b>, French troops occupied the territory in February 1923, ending its separate existence.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-bottleneck">Free State Bottleneck</a></li></ul></section>
